		<category><![CDATA[Google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google DeepMind]]></category>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://ventureburn.com/?p=204097</guid>

					<description><![CDATA[<p>Google is making its biggest AI leadership change in years. The company shakes up AI leadership and still maintains upbeat memos. On Wednesday, Google shakes up AI leadership, announcing that</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/google-ai-leadership-shuffle-hassabis-jeff-dean/" data-wpel-link="internal">Google Shakes Up AI Leadership, Demis Hassabis Steps Down As DeepMind CEO</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Google is making its biggest AI leadership change in years. The company shakes up AI leadership and still maintains upbeat memos. On Wednesday, Google shakes up AI leadership, announcing that Demis Hassabis would step down as CEO of </span><a href="https://deepmind.google/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">Google DeepMind</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> to become chairman of the unit and chief scientist for parent company Alphabet.  </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Jeff Dean, Google’s chief scientist and a 27-year veteran, said he is leaving to start a new company with three other senior Google AI colleagues. Koray Kavukcuoglu, who has been DeepMind’s CTO, will take over as senior vice president of the division, reporting directly to CEO Sundar Pichai.</span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">Google Faces Pressure from Rivals</span></h2>
<p><a href="https://ventureburn.com/tech-giants-commit-265-million-on-electricians-data-centers/" data-wpel-link="internal"><span style="font-weight: 400">Google</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> said the two moves were not connected. But the timing speaks to the pressure the company is under. Gemini 3.5 Pro is reportedly months behind schedule, while competitors OpenAI and Anthropic have released new models that push the boundaries of what AI can do. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Several top researchers have also walked out the door, including Gemini’s co-lead. Inside Google, there has been more friction too. Multiple employees said publicly that they resigned after Google signed an April deal with the Pentagon that lets the military use its technology.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Outside the company, the reaction was swift and blunt. Former Googler Peter J. Liu called the changes “hard to understate” in a now-deleted post on X, pointing out that both Gemini leadership and Hassabis had stepped back from day-to-day roles. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Dan Shipper, CEO of AI subscription service Every, said Google now needs to “catch up on frontier coding” and suggested Hassabis was more focused on long-term research like world models than on shipping products this quarter. </span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/moove-250-million-to-lead-robotaxi-industry/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Moove Raises $250M To Lead the Robotaxi Industry</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">Google’s Response To the Growing AI Competition</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Inside Google, the tone was different. Pichai, Hassabis and Kavukcuoglu all released upbeat memos. Pichai praised Google’s talent, compute, and product reach, but also said the company must “accelerate all this work and stay focused on the AI frontier.” </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Kavukcuoglu has been closer to recent model releases than Hassabis and is seen internally as someone who can navigate Google’s complex politics. Still, the shift matters for how DeepMind operates. The unit was once run as a largely independent lab. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Now, with no CEO and a senior VP reporting to Pichai, some DeepMind staff see it as a further loss of autonomy. Losing both Hassabis and Dean also removes two of the most respected figures in Google’s AI history. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Hassabis, a Nobel laureate, was viewed as a steady steward for Google’s ambitions as AI grows more powerful. Dean has been central to Google’s AI work since the early days.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/hadrian-raises-1-37-billion-us-defense-production/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Hadrian Raises $1.37B for US Defense Production</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">What’s Next?</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Despite the uproar, Google still has major advantages. It owns its own chips and cloud infrastructure, and its core businesses in Search, Ads, and Cloud generate the cash needed to fund massive AI spending. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">It also has AI products reaching billions of users. The leadership changes suggest a strategic pivot. Google appears to be moving from a research-first posture to one focused on shipping and competing. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">With OpenAI and Anthropic setting a fast pace, that may be exactly what Google needs. But with talent leaving and expectations rising, there’s really no room for delays. </span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/river-mobility-raises-120-million-to-scale-indias-utility-ev-market/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>River Mobility Raises $120M To Scale India’s Utility EV Market</i></b></a></p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/google-ai-leadership-shuffle-hassabis-jeff-dean/" data-wpel-link="internal">Google Shakes Up AI Leadership, Demis Hassabis Steps Down As DeepMind CEO</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
					
		
		
			</item>
