					<description><![CDATA[<p>Africa just got a new mega-unicorn, and it’s not selling e-commerce or fintech apps. The mobility fintech Moove raises $250 million in Series C to scale robotaxis. Led by Tiger</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://ventureburn.com/moove-250-million-to-lead-robotaxi-industry/" data-wpel-link="internal">Moove Raises $250 Million To Lead the Robotaxi Industry</a> appeared first on <a href="https://ventureburn.com" data-wpel-link="internal">Ventureburn</a>.</p>
]]></description>
										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Africa just got a new mega-unicorn, and it’s not selling e-commerce or fintech apps. The mobility fintech Moove </span><a href="https://www.moove.io/drive-to-own/news/moove-series-c" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener external noreferrer" data-wpel-link="external"><span style="font-weight: 400">raises</span></a><span style="font-weight: 400"> $250 million in Series C to scale robotaxis. Led by Tiger Global, the round has pushed its valuation to $2.1 billion. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Existing backers Uber, Mubadala, BlackRock, and Prosus Ventures didn’t miss out on the opportunity. The capital will be used to scale autonomous vehicle operations, double down on AI, and expand into more global markets.</span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">The Loan Problem That Moove Solves</span></h2>
<div id="attachment_204093" style="width: 712px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-204093" class="size-full wp-image-204093" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Moove.jpg" alt="A branded Moove hatchback vehicle, highlighted as Uber’s preferred fleet partner in Sub-Saharan Africa." width="702" height="439" /><p id="caption-attachment-204093" class="wp-caption-text">Moove Fleet Vehicle</p></div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Moove launched in 2020 in Lagos with one insight: millions of ride-hailing drivers couldn’t get car loans. Banks wanted credit scores and drivers didn’t have them. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">So, Moove flipped the model. Instead of credit scores, they used earnings data. If you were driving and making money on Uber, Bolt, or others, Moove would finance your car and take repayments from your trips. It worked. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The company scaled across Africa fast. Then it went further across Europe, Middle East, India, and the US. But lending was only the first chapter for Moove. </span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/hadrian-raises-1-37-billion-us-defense-production/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>Hadrian Raises $1.37B for US Defense Production</i></b></a><b><i> </i></b></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">From Financing Cars to Managing Robotaxi Fleets</span></h2>
<div id="attachment_204092" style="width: 1034px" class="wp-caption aligncenter"><img decoding="async" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-204092" class="size-large wp-image-204092" src="https://ventureburn.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/Moove-operational-layer-1024x639.jpg" alt="Moove expands its platform infrastructure to support the deployment and operations of autonomous mobility fleets." width="1024" height="639" /><p id="caption-attachment-204092" class="wp-caption-text">Operational Layer for Autonomous Mobility: Moove</p></div>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Today, Moove doesn’t just leave things at car financing. It additionally buys the cars, handles maintenance, insurance, and day-to-day operations, and uses all the trip data to train their AI. Early investors backed a lender for drivers. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">This round backs a company trying to power the future of autonomous transport. CEO Ladi Delano hasn’t said it out loud, but the bet is clear. As more cities move to self-driving cars, someone has to own and operate the fleets. Moove wants to be that operator.</span></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">The Numbers Behind the Moove Leap</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">This growth didn’t happen in a vacuum. In March 2024, there was a $100M raise at a $750M valuation, led by Uber. In September 2025, reports surfaced of a new raise above $2B as revenues accelerated. Now in August 2026, Moove has raised $250M at a $2.1 billion valuation—a target hit in less than a year.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Along the way, Moove grew aggressively. It began managing fleets in Brazil and deepened its Waymo partnership in the US. By default, revenue skyrocketed as the company moved beyond ride-hailing finance into broader mobility services.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Here’s the story investors are buying: data from millions of trips plus operational expertise running cars equals the right to manage AV fleets at scale.</span></p>
<p><b><i>More Updates: </i></b><a href="https://ventureburn.com/river-mobility-raises-120-million-to-scale-indias-utility-ev-market/" data-wpel-link="internal"><b><i>River Mobility Raises $120M To Scale India’s Utility EV Market</i></b></a></p>
<h2><span style="font-weight: 400">The Hard Part Starts Now</span></h2>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">Valuation is one thing. Execution is another. Self-driving cars are still new and costly. Expanding globally means navigating US and EU regulations, managing fleets, and keeping vehicles running 24/7 with barely any downtime.</span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">It also means massive capital. That’s why Moove needed $250M and will probably need more later. But if they get it right, the returns are huge. </span></p>
<p><span style="font-weight: 400">The world will need operators who can manage thousands of self-driving cars across dozens of cities. If Moove becomes one of those operators, it won’t just be an African success story. It will be a global one.</span></p>
